{{- /*
  support/ask-redirects — owner-ask step for the OPTIONAL `redirects.md`
  fragment. Owner: `Support: investigate`.

  Rendered by the owner prompt when the agent judges that out-of-scope
  redirects are relevant for this channel and
  `.mitto/support/<channel-id>/redirects.md` does not yet exist. Because
  this fragment is optional, the owner may skip asking entirely; when it
  does ask, it uses this template.

  Fields on the `dict` argument:

    TimeoutSeconds:  int. Interactive owners pass 300; silent owners a
                     short value.

  Whitespace: fragment body starts flush at column 1 (no leading `- `) and
  emits no trailing newline; callers use plain `{{ template ... }}`.
*/ -}}
{{- $timeout := .TimeoutSeconds -}}
**Ask + write `redirects.md`.** Open a focused `mitto_ui_form`
  (`timeout_seconds: {{ $timeout }}`) titled "Channel redirects — where do out-of-scope questions
  belong?" with these fields (all optional, but at least one should be filled in):
  - `topic_map` (textarea) — "Topic → destination map: for each out-of-scope topic, name the
    correct channel / team / contact. One per line, e.g. 'billing questions → #billing-help'."
  - `boilerplate` (textarea, optional) — "Boilerplate redirect message(s) we can adapt when
    politely pointing a customer elsewhere. Keep them short and friendly."
  - `notes` (textarea, optional) — "Anything else relevant to redirects (do-not-redirect topics,
    exceptions)."

  When the form returns, create `.mitto/support/<channel-id>/` if it does not exist, then write the
  result to `.mitto/support/<channel-id>/redirects.md` as a Markdown document with sections
  `# Topic map`, `# Boilerplate` (only if non-empty), and `# Notes` (only if non-empty). Confirm
  the write via `mitto_ui_notify` (success). If the form times out or is aborted, do **not** write
  a partial file — fall back per the caller's policy (this fragment is optional; skip silently is
  acceptable).
{{- /* no trailing newline */ -}}
